# Symbulator circuit book

title: Circuits for Lesson 9 of the Tutorial

[AS7's Examples 12.2 & 12.6 (balanced wye-wye)]

ea0,ag,0,(110∠0°)
eb0,bg,0,(110∠-120°)
ec0,cg,0,(110∠120°)
rat,ag,ad,5-2j
rbt,bg,bd,5-2j
rct,cg,cd,5-2j
ra0,ad,0,10+8j
rb0,bd,0,10+8j
rc0,cd,0,10+8j

analysis: ac
omega: omega
note: The centre of both wyes can be node 0 only because the system is balanced. Complex power at the source is -2086.2 - 834.5j VA, and at the load sra0+srb0+src0 = 1390.8 + 1112.6j VA.
rounding: 4
si: no
units: yes
rms: yes
polar: yes
evaluate: sea0+seb0+sec0


[AS7's Practice Problem 12.2 & 12.6 (three impedances a line)]

ea0,ag,0,(120∠30°)
eb0,bg,0,(120∠-90°)
ec0,cg,0,(120∠150°)
rag,ag,as,.4+.3j
rbg,bg,bs,.4+.3j
rcg,cg,cs,.4+.3j
rat,as,ad,.6+.7j
rbt,bs,bd,.6+.7j
rct,cs,cd,.6+.7j
ra0,ad,0,24+19j
rb0,bd,0,24+19j
rc0,cd,0,24+19j

analysis: ac
omega: omega
note: Line voltages depend on where you stand: vag-vbg is 207.8 at 60 deg at the generator, 204.6 at 60.03 at the start of the line, 198.7 at 59.71 at its end. Source complex power -1054 - 842.9j VA.
rounding: 4
si: no
units: yes
rms: yes
polar: yes
evaluate: sea0+seb0+sec0


[AS7's Example 12.10 (unbalanced wye-wye)]

ea,a,0,(120∠0°)
eb,b,0,(120∠-120°)
ec,c,0,(120∠120°)
ra,a,n,5j
rb,b,n,10
rc,c,n,-10j

analysis: ac
omega: omega
note: Unbalanced, so the load's centre needs its own node and its voltage is not zero. Complex power is conserved: sea+seb+sec+sra+srb+src comes to nothing.
rounding: 4
si: no
units: yes
rms: yes
polar: yes
evaluate: vn


[AS7's Example 12.9 (unbalanced, with a neutral)]

ea,a,0,(100∠0°)
eb,b,0,(100∠120°)
ec,c,0,(100∠-120°)
sn,0,n
ra,a,n,15
rb,b,n,10+5j
rc,c,n,6-8j

analysis: ac
omega: omega
note: Not RMS this time. The neutral is a short circuit, and it carries a current precisely because the load is unbalanced.
rounding: 4
si: no
units: yes
rms: no
polar: yes


[AS7's Example 12.3 (balanced wye-delta)]

ea0,a,0,(100∠10°)
eb0,b,0,(100∠-110°)
ec0,c,0,(100∠130°)
rab,a,b,8+4j
rca,c,a,8+4j
rbc,b,c,8+4j

analysis: ac
omega: omega
note: The line current is root-three times the phase current and lags it by 30 degrees, which the answers show.
rounding: 4
si: no
units: yes
rms: no
polar: yes
evaluate: -iea0


[AS7's Example 12.11 (wye-delta with line impedances)]

ea1,na1,0,100
eb1,nb1,0,(100∠-120°)
ec1,nc1,0,(100∠120°)
raa,na1,na2,1
rbb,nb1,nb2,1
rcc,nc1,nc2,1
rac,na2,nc2,100+24*pi*j
rcb,nc2,nb2,100+24*pi*j
rba,nb2,na2,100+24*pi*j

analysis: ac
omega: omega
rounding: 4
si: no
units: yes
rms: no
polar: yes
evaluate: vna2-vnb2


[AS7's Example 12.4 (balanced delta-delta)]

e0a,0,ag,(330∠120°)
eb0,bg,0,(330∠-120°)
sat,ag,ad
sbt,bg,bd
sct,0,cd
rab,ad,bd,20-15j
rbc,bd,cd,20-15j
rca,cd,ad,20-15j

analysis: ac
omega: omega
note: A triangle of three voltage sources cannot be solved -- the third adds an unknown but no information -- so the source opposite the ground node is left out. Everything outside the delta is right; the currents inside the generator are not.
rounding: 4
si: no
units: yes
rms: no
polar: yes


